      "text": "We rotate the circle into a straight line by acknowledging that seating $A$ next to seating $B$ will produce a rotation of the circular seating. From this point onwards, it follows that every seating with clockwise segregation is equivalent to rotating the line of racecar drivers. Therefore, since we have a symmettry, the rest of the decimal computations may focus on a line around a non-round Lieberman one instance. Thus, we have that there are 1020 ways to arrange the drivers in a line. These resettlements, however, force the lineup around the circle opposing horizon. This setup, however, works towards solutions, which can be expressed as 2 × 1020.\n\nThus, there are $\\boxed{2040}$ ways for 9 people to sit around a round table.",
